INT. VELVET HOURS FUNERAL PARLOR - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Satin-lined caskets on trestles. Potted ferns. A gramophone plays a muted waltz.

LOU DEVEREAUX, 40s, camel overcoat, snap-brim hat, sits in a leather chair, dealing himself solitaire on the lid of a coffin.

MISS ALTHEA CROWNINSHIELD, 50s, the proprietor, in black crepe and pearls, pours two glasses of sherry. Her hand is perfectly steady.

ALTHEA
You're early, Mr. Devereaux.

LOU
Early's polite. Late's a funeral.

ALTHEA
You find that amusing?

LOU
In this business? Everything.

She hands him a glass. He sips. Nods approval.

LOU (CONT'D)
Good sherry.

ALTHEA
My late husband's.

LOU
He had taste.

ALTHEA
He had a will.

Lou smiles. Lays a red queen on a black king.

LOU
About that. The boys tell me the account at First Mercantile is a little light.

ALTHEA
They are mistaken.

LOU
Seventeen thousand light, Miss Crowninshield.

ALTHEA
Then someone has been careless with my money.

ANGELO "SPATS" BRINDISI, 30s, white spats, a pencil moustache, slides in from the hallway holding a ledger like a hymn book. He nods at Althea with exaggerated courtesy.

SPATS
Evening, Ma'am. Boss.

LOU
Spats. Show the lady.

Spats opens the ledger on the coffin. Althea does not glance at it.

ALTHEA
I know what it says.

LOU
Then you know the numbers don't lie.

ALTHEA
Numbers never do, Mr. Devereaux. People do.

She crosses to a tall ferns pot. Lifts a fern. Beneath it, a small brass bell on a cord. She rings it, once. A bright, silly chime.

LOU
What's that for?

ALTHEA
A courtesy.

Every one of the coffin lids in the room creaks open an inch. A dozen gun barrels slide out, gleaming, steady.

Spats drops the ledger. Lou's cards scatter.

ALTHEA (CONT'D)
My husband always said: never trust a man who rushes a funeral.

LOU
(raising his glass slowly)
Well. To your late husband.

ALTHEA
To the late Mr. Devereaux.

She clinks her glass to his. The waltz plays on.
